"Your organisation" means the organisation you accept these terms for (or, if you accept them only for yourself, you), together with its related bodies corporate (as defined in section 50 of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth)). Your organisation may use the software under these terms, and you are responsible for its compliance with them. References to your own servers, applications, templates, data, customers and staff include those of your organisation.

## 1. What these terms cover

They cover the Community edition of Frontforge Report Server: the container image, the programs in it (reportd and renderd), the compose bundle, the studio and viewer they serve, and the documentation that comes with them, as Frontforge releases them (the "software").

The software needs a licence to run, and the Community edition's licence is free. You get a Community licence from us at https://myaccount.frontforge.com, with an email address, and give it to the software when you set it up. A Community licence never expires. These terms cover the software used with a Community licence.

You accept these terms by ticking "I accept" in the setup wizard. These terms are also shown in the README and included in the container image. If you install, copy, deploy or use the software without the setup wizard (for example with the compose bundle, or in a headless or automated deployment), you accept these terms by doing so. If you don't accept them, don't install or use it.

## 2. What you may do

We grant you a free, non-exclusive, non-transferable, worldwide licence to install and use the software, subject to these terms, commercial use included. That includes designing reports, rendering documents from your own data, and giving those documents to your own customers, staff and anyone else, through your own applications or otherwise. It also includes rendering documents from your own templates inside your own applications where your users or customers trigger the rendering and receive the documents.

You may make the copies you need to do that: on your own servers, in your own private image registries, in backups, and for contractors who run the software for you under these terms. You are responsible for your contractors' compliance with these terms, and for their acts and omissions in connection with the software, as if they were your own.

## 3. The Community limits

The Community limits are the ones set in the release of the software you use. For this release, the Community edition allows 5 templates, 1 sub-report per template and 3 designer seats, and the features the software makes available with a Community licence. The software enforces those limits.

A "designer seat" is one named user who can design or edit templates in the studio.

A licence for a paid edition lifts the Community limits. A paid edition is used under its own agreement with us, not under these terms.

## 4. What you may not do

You may not:

- get around the Community limits or the licence checks, or change the software to do so;
- offer a Hosted Reporting Service;
- sell, rent, lend or sublicense the software, or publish or distribute copies of it to others (for example in a public registry, or inside a product you distribute), except as section 2 allows;
- reverse engineer, decompile or disassemble the software, except as far as the law allows despite this limit, or as section 6 allows;
- remove or change the copyright, licence or attribution notices in the software;
- use the software in breach of any law, including export control and sanctions laws that apply to you.

A "Hosted Reporting Service" is any service or product, run by you or anyone else, that makes the studio, the designer or template management in the software available to anyone outside your organisation, or that lets anyone outside your organisation design, upload or manage their own templates.

This section doesn't stop you rendering documents from your own templates inside your own applications, including where your users or customers trigger the rendering and receive the documents.

The law that this section can't limit includes sections 47D to 47F of the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth), which allow some copying of computer programs for interoperability, error correction and security testing. Section 47H of that Act gives no effect to any agreement that excludes or limits them.

## 5. Your content is yours

Your templates, data, and the documents the software produces from them are yours. We claim no rights in them, and the software doesn't send them to us.

The examples in the example gallery are there to be used: you may copy, change and share them, and anything you make from them, without restriction.

## 6. Open source components

The software includes open source components, each under its own licence. `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`, which ships with the software, lists them with their licences. Nothing in these terms limits what those licences allow you to do with those components. Where a component's licence requires it (for example, the GNU Lesser General Public License), you may modify and reverse engineer that component as that licence allows. The fonts bundled with the software are under the SIL Open Font Licence.

## 7. What the software sends

The software sends no telemetry, and checks its licence on your own server, without contacting us. On its own, it makes two kinds of requests to the internet:

- **The version check:** once a day, reportd asks GitHub for the latest Frontforge Report Server release, to tell administrators when a newer one is out. The request carries no version, install ID or usage data.
- **The example gallery:** at most once an hour, reportd reads the gallery's list from GitHub, and downloads an example when someone chooses it.

GitHub sees the address of the server making the request, as with any request. Both can be turned off in the settings: `updates.check` (which the setup wizard also offers) and `examples.gallery`.

The software also connects to services an administrator sets up in it, such as data sources, font sources, sign-in providers and webhooks, and only once they are set up.

This section describes the requests this release makes. A future release may make different requests. If it does, we'll describe them in that release's terms.

